// Runtime/ServiceLoop.maxon — `<T>.__loop`, the dispatch loop the compiler synthesizes for every SERVICE
// (SV1 wave 3). Designed in `SERVICES_DESIGN.md` §"What the compiler synthesizes".
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS BUILT AT THE MAXON TIER, ON `ModuleInit.SynthesizedFunction`, AND THE TIER IS THE WHOLE
// WIRING.** The loop CALLS USER CODE — one handler per message, with the receiver, the argument slotting,
// the ranged-alias widths and the dual-register error ABI a real call has — so building it as already-lowered
// `StdOp`s would be a second copy of `lowerCall`, which is the duplication this compiler pays for hardest.
// Appended to the merged module beside `__module_init`, it is ordinary program code to everything below:
// `SemanticCheck` type-checks its calls, `scanRuntimeUsage` sees its `__mbox_recv` / `__mm_decref` /
// `__destruct_<T>` calls and installs the floor they need, and `lowerMaxonToStd` lowers it like anything
// else.
//
// ⭐ **ONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING, BECAUSE IT DELETES A WHOLE OBLIGATION.** `AsyncReleaser` has to
// DECLARE what its bodies will call (`declareAsyncReleaserNeeds`) because they are Std-tier bodies built
// AFTER the runtime-needs scan — rule 2 of `RuntimeUsage`'s header. This loop is built BEFORE that scan, so
// every drop it emits is DISCOVERED: the request union's cascade, each payload's drop and the state's are
// rooted by the ordinary walk, and the two `funcAddr`s `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerServiceSpawn` emits name
// symbols the loop itself already calls.
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **EVERY TYPE THIS FILE RESOLVES OUT OF THE SIGNATURE INDEX STAYS IN THE INDEX'S NAME SPACE, AND
// `SynthesizedFunction.mint` IS WHAT CROSSES IT (SV1 wave 4).** *"Names travel between interners; ids never
// do"* — an id is a number, and it means whatever name sits at that number in the table you read it against.
// The carrier's `projectScopedNameId` crosses every id a MINT is given, so a caller that hands it one which
// is ALREADY project-scoped gets a SECOND crossing, from the wrong side: the number is resolved through the
// index, lands on an unrelated name, and that name is re-interned into the project.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED, and it is the lexer keyword map's `expected 'KeywordInfo', got 'SpillSlot'` a second time.**
// This file resolved its payload types `into: project.typeNames` and then minted from them. On a program
// with two services where one message takes a `String` its handler calls a method on and another takes a
// `Logger.handle`, the tables had diverged by exactly one entry and the loop's handle argument came out as
// **`E3005 argument type mismatch for 'sink': expected 'Logger.handle', got 'ExitCode'`** — a clean refusal
// of a correct program, with no file or line, because the check was walking THIS synthesized body. The
// diagnostic is the mild face of it: the same misread id also chooses `classifyUnionPayload`'s class and
// `managedFieldDropCallee`'s callee, which is a payload dropped through the wrong destructor or not at all.
// It stayed invisible for a rung because the two tables agree at every id until enough types are declared to
// push them apart, and every existing service case was under that threshold.
//
// ⇒ **`signatures.indexTypeNames()` at every one of those doors, and `project.typeNames` at exactly two:**
// `stateType`, which is interned by NAME rather than read out of the index and is handed to
// `SynthesizedFunction.open` (which stores parameter ids RAW — see the carrier), and the drop callee that
// pairs with it. A `typeNames:` argument and the `MaxonType` beside it must always be one table.
//
// ⭐⭐ **NO `__do_<m>` TRAMPOLINES, AND THE DESIGN DOCUMENT SAYS OTHERWISE — SO READ THIS RATHER THAN IT.**
// `SERVICES_DESIGN.md` reasoned that each dispatch arm had to be a single call because **E2049** admits only
// one statement per `match` arm. That is a SOURCE rule (`Parser.parseArmBody`,
// `Parser.isBlockOpeningStatement`) and this loop is not source: at the IR tier a `switch` arm is a BLOCK,
// which holds as many ops as it likes. So there is ONE function per service and the handler call, the drops
// and the shell decref all live in the arm — which is also what makes the un-consumed-payload drop
// expressible at all.

// ⭐⭐ **BUILD `<T>.__loop` FOR EVERY SERVICE IN THE PROGRAM.** Called from `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`
// directly after `installManagedGlobalInit`, at the same tier and for the same reason — see this file's
// header, and `ModuleInit`'s, which states the position's whole argument.
//
// A program that spawns nothing builds NOTHING here: `serviceTypeRoster` is empty, which is every program
// that never writes `spawn`.
export function installServiceLoops(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	let services = serviceTypeRoster(signatures)
	// ⭐ **ONE WALK OF THE MODULE FOR EVERY SERVICE, NOT ONE PER SERVICE.** The walk is Θ(program), so
	// driving it from inside the roster loop would multiply the whole module by the number of service types
	// — the one place in this file where a per-type constant would become a per-type PASS.
	let spawned = spawnedInstancesByService(module, services: services)
	for i in 0 upto services.count() 'eachService'
		let typeName = try services.get(i) otherwise panic("installServiceLoops: services.get OOB at {i} on a list walked to its own count")
		buildServiceLoops(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, typeName: typeName, instances: try spawned.get(i) otherwise panic("installServiceLoops: spawned.get OOB at {i} — one bucket is pushed per service before the walk"))
		buildServiceAbandon(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, typeName: typeName)
	end 'eachService'
end 'installServiceLoops'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LOOP IS MONOMORPHIC WHERE THE COMPANIONS ARE NOT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT A GENERIC
// SERVICE COSTS.** One `<T>.request` and one `<T>.handle` serve every instantiation — neither carries a type
// argument — but the loop CALLS the handlers and DROPS the state, and both of those are per-instance facts:
// a `returns T` handler takes the instance's layout descriptor, and the state releases through
// `__destruct_<mangled>`. A shared body has nothing to source either from (a synthesized function carries no
// dictionary of its own), so there is one loop per instantiation the program spawns.
//
// ⚠ **`<T>.__abandon` STAYS PER-TYPE**, and can: it walks the request UNION, whose payloads can never name a
// type parameter — `ServiceCompanions.untransferableParameterNoun` refuses one at the `spawn`.
function buildServiceLoops(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, instances GenericInstanceIdArray)
	if not signatures.structDeclaresTypeParams(typeName) 'monomorphic'
		buildServiceLoop(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, typeName: typeName, instance: ServiceStateInstance.monomorphic)
		return
	end 'monomorphic'

	// ⛔⛔ **AN EMPTY BUCKET BUILDS NOTHING AND IS NOT AN ASSERTION FAILURE** — the discipline
	// `ServiceCompanions.mintServiceReplyErrorType` states for the sweep, and for its reason. The ROSTER is
	// built from the discovery walk's raw token candidates, which know nothing of any refusal; a `ParseError`
	// anywhere in a file discards that file's whole artifact (`Queries.queryFileParse`), so a doomed program
	// reaches here with its type on the roster and not one `serviceSpawn` op behind it. There is nothing to
	// build a loop for and nothing that could call one.
	for gid in instances 'eachInstantiation'
		buildServiceLoop(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, typeName: typeName, instance: ServiceStateInstance.at(gid))
	end 'eachInstantiation'
end 'buildServiceLoops'

// One bucket of spawned instantiations per entry of `services`, in that order.
typealias SpawnedInstanceBuckets = Array with GenericInstanceIdArray

// Which instantiations the walk has already bucketed. A gid determines its own base, so one set serves
// every service and no gid can land in two buckets.
typealias SpawnedInstanceSet = Set with GenericInstanceId

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY INSTANTIATION THIS PROGRAM ACTUALLY SPAWNS, READ OFF THE OPS, IN ONE WALK.** The index knows
// every instance the program NAMES, which is a larger set — an instance nothing spawns would get a loop
// whose handler calls and state drop nothing ever reaches, and whose reply the `spawn` road never judged
// (`ServiceCompanions.uncarriableReplyResultNoun` refuses a reply the cell cannot carry, and it is asked at
// the `spawn`). The `serviceSpawn` ops are the exact set, and their state values are read through the same
// classifier `LowerMaxonToStd.spawnedStateInstance` uses, so the loop that is BUILT is the loop that is
// CALLED.
//
// ⚠ **THE ROSTER LOOKUP INSIDE THE WALK IS OVER DISTINCT SERVICE TYPES**, which is a count an author writes
// by hand (`serviceTypeRoster`'s own bound) — unlike the INSTANTIATIONS, which grow with the program and are
// therefore deduped through a set rather than a scan.
function spawnedInstancesByService(module MaxonModule, services ByteArrayArray) returns SpawnedInstanceBuckets
	var buckets = SpawnedInstanceBuckets.create()
	for _ in services 'eachService'
		buckets.push(GenericInstanceIdArray.create())
	end 'eachService'

	var seen = SpawnedInstanceSet.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("spawnedInstancesByService: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				noteSpawnedInstance(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("spawnedInstancesByService: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), func: func, services: services, buckets: buckets, seen: seen)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return buckets
end 'spawnedInstancesByService'

function noteSpawnedInstance(op MaxonOp, func IrFunction, services ByteArrayArray, buckets SpawnedInstanceBuckets, seen SpawnedInstanceSet)
	match op 'op'
		serviceSpawn(_, spawnedType, state) then bucketSpawnedInstance(func, spawnedType: spawnedType, state: state, services: services, buckets: buckets, seen: seen)
		// Every other op starts no service. Two ranges rather than one because `serviceSpawn` sits between
		// `errorFlagEncode` and `serviceSend` in the dialect's own order, and the second runs to the union's
		// LAST variant — a variant appended past it would otherwise land outside both ranges, which is the
		// band-append invariant doing its job rather than a range to widen without reading.
		call to errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		serviceSend to immortalStringTable then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteSpawnedInstance'

// One `serviceSpawn`'s instantiation, into its service's bucket. A state standing at NO instantiation is a
// monomorphic service's plain box and is bucketed by nobody — `buildServiceLoops` is where that is judged.
function bucketSpawnedInstance(func IrFunction, spawnedType ByteArray, state ValueId, services ByteArrayArray, buckets SpawnedInstanceBuckets, seen SpawnedInstanceSet)
	let tag = try func.valueTags.get(state) otherwise panic("bucketSpawnedInstance: v{state} is not in '{String.from(func.name)}'s dense value-tag column")
	let nameId = try func.valueNameIds.get(state) otherwise panic("bucketSpawnedInstance: v{state} is not in '{String.from(func.name)}'s dense value-name column")
	match serviceStateInstanceOfValueType(tag, nameId: nameId) 'stands'
		monomorphic then return
		at(gid) then noteDistinctInstance(gid, spawnedType: spawnedType, services: services, buckets: buckets, seen: seen)
	end 'stands'
end 'bucketSpawnedInstance'

function noteDistinctInstance(gid GenericInstanceId, spawnedType ByteArray, services ByteArrayArray, buckets SpawnedInstanceBuckets, seen SpawnedInstanceSet)
	if seen.contains(gid) 'alreadyBucketed'
		return
	end 'alreadyBucketed'

	for i in 0 upto services.count() 'eachService'
		let listed = try services.get(i) otherwise panic("noteDistinctInstance: services.get OOB at {i} on a list walked to its own count")
		if not listed.equals(spawnedType) 'anotherService'
			continue
		end 'anotherService'

		var bucket = try buckets.get(i) otherwise panic("noteDistinctInstance: buckets.get OOB at {i} — one bucket is pushed per service before the walk")
		seen.insert(gid)
		bucket.push(gid)
		return
	end 'eachService'

	// ⭐⭐ **FALLING OUT IS THE ANSWER FOR A SPAWN OF A TYPE THE ROSTER DOES NOT CARRY, AND IT IS NOT THE
	// LINK FAILURE THIS FILE'S HEADER WARNS OF.** That warning is about a type on ONE of the two lists: a
	// handle whose loop nothing built. This is the other direction — an op whose type the roster declined —
	// and the roster declines exactly the targets `Parser.parseSpawn` refuses at the source
	// (`serviceTypeRoster` skips every verdict but `startsAService`). A program holding such an op is one
	// the parse has already rejected, so building nothing for it is what leaves the author with their own
	// diagnostic instead of a second finding about its wreckage.
end 'noteDistinctInstance'

// The loop's two parameters, by the ValueId the parameter numbering reserves for each — `ModuleInit`'s
// `ExitCodeParam` shape, and for its reason: a synthesized function has no parameter NAMES, so argument `n`
// is a named constant or it is a bare number at every use.
let ServiceStateParam = 0 as ValueId
let ServiceMailboxParam = 1 as ValueId

// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE LOOP, and its shape is the answer to "what does a service DO?".**
//
// ```
// loop:      req = __mbox_recv(mailbox)        // parks when the queue is empty and the mailbox is open
//            req == 0 -> exit                  // closed AND drained: nobody can ever send again
//            switch loadIndirect(req, tag)
//              case 0 (__shutdown) -> drop the pill, exit
//              case k              -> load each payload, call <T>.<m>(state, …), drop what the
//                                     handler did not consume, __mm_decref the shell, loop
// exit:      __mbox_close(mailbox)             // idempotent; makes the queue drainable and un-fillable
// drain:     leftover = __mbox_recv(mailbox)   // closed now, so this never parks
//            leftover != 0 -> abandon it through the request union's own cascade, drain again
//            drop state
//            __mbox_service_exit(mailbox)
// ```
//
// ⭐ **THE DRAIN IS `__mbox_recv` AGAIN, NOT A SECOND WALK OF THE QUEUE.** Once `closed` is set that entry
// pops what is there and answers 0 when there is nothing left — which is exactly the drain's question — so
// the queue's pop protocol is written once and a message abandoned after a `shutdown` takes the same road a
// handled one does, minus the handler.
//
// ⚠ **THE `__shutdown` ARM DROPS THE PILL AND LEAVES; IT DOES NOT DRAIN.** Draining is the exit path's, and
// both roads into `exit` go through it — so "shutdown drains what was queued behind the pill" and "the last
// handle drop drains what was queued" are one piece of code rather than two that could disagree.
function buildServiceLoop(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, instance ServiceStateInstance)
	// ⭐ **THE STATE PARAMETER'S TYPE IS WHERE THE WHOLE PER-INSTANCE ABI COMES FROM.** A `genericInstance`
	// here is what makes `MaxonToStdLowering.materializeLayoutArg` hand each handler `__layout_<mangled>` and
	// `managedFieldDropCallee` hand the drain `__destruct_<mangled>` — both read argument 0's own type, so
	// naming the instantiation once here supplies every dictionary this body needs.
	let stateType = match instance 'i'
		monomorphic gives MaxonType.structRef(project.typeNames.intern(typeName))
		at(id) gives MaxonType.genericInstance(id)
	end 'i'
	var paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	paramTypes.push(stateType)
	paramTypes.push(MaxonType.integer)
	var fn = SynthesizedFunction.open(module, project: project, name: serviceLoopSymbolFor(signatures, typeName: typeName, instance: instance), returnType: MaxonReturnType.void, paramTypes: paramTypes)

	let range = SourceRange.none()
	let request = requireServiceRequestLayout(signatures, typeName: typeName)

	let entry = fn.currentBlock()
	let receive = fn.openBlock(b"serve")
	let req = fn.emitPositionalCall(MboxRecvName, args: argsOf(ServiceMailboxParam), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	let noRequest = fn.emitCompare(req, rhs: fn.emitTypedLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(entry, op: MaxonOp.branch(receive.id), range: range)

	let dispatch = fn.openBlock(b"dispatch")
	let tag = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(tag, addr: req, offset: UnionTagOffset, valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)

	// The exit path is opened BEFORE the arms so every arm has a block id to branch to, and the arms are the
	// switch's dense table in TAG order — `request.tagOfIndex(i)` is `i` by construction
	// (`ServiceCompanions.addServiceRequestCase` derives each tag from the case count), so the table's index
	// IS the case index.
	let exit = fn.openBlock(b"stopping")
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(MboxCloseName, args: argsOf(ServiceMailboxParam), range: range)

	var armIds = BlockIdArray.create()
	for caseIndex in 0 upto request.caseCount() 'eachVariant'
		armIds.push(buildServiceArm(fn, signatures: signatures, typeName: typeName, instance: instance, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, req: req, receive: receive, exit: exit, range: range).id)
	end 'eachVariant'

	// ⚠ **THE DEFAULT IS THE EXIT, NOT AN ABORT, AND THAT IS A CHOICE ABOUT A CASE THAT CANNOT HAPPEN.** The
	// table covers every tag the union has and only `<T>.__request` construction can fill a tag word, so the
	// default is unreachable by construction. It goes to the exit rather than to a `panic` because a
	// `RuntimeAbort` there would be a claim about a corrupted heap that this loop is in no position to make —
	// and stopping the service is the conservative answer to "a request I do not recognise".
	fn.terminateBlock(dispatch, op: MaxonOp.switch(tag, denseTargetBlockIds: armIds, defaultBlockId: exit.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(receive, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(noRequest, thenBlockId: exit.id, elseBlockId: dispatch.id), range: range)

	buildServiceDrain(fn, project: project, signatures: signatures, stateType: stateType, exit: exit, range: range)
end 'buildServiceLoop'

// The exit path: drain whatever is still queued through the request union's own cascade, drop the state, and
// hand back this green thread's own ticket. `exit` has already closed the mailbox.
function buildServiceDrain(fn SynthesizedFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stateType MaxonType, exit IrBlock, range SourceRange)
	let drain = fn.openBlock(b"drain")
	let leftover = fn.emitPositionalCall(MboxRecvName, args: argsOf(ServiceMailboxParam), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	let drained = fn.emitCompare(leftover, rhs: fn.emitTypedLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(exit, op: MaxonOp.branch(drain.id), range: range)

	// ⭐ **AN ABANDONED REQUEST TAKES `<T>.__abandon`, WHERE A HANDLED ONE TAKES A SHELL DECREF — and the
	// difference is which of them still owns its payloads AND whether anybody is waiting on it.** A handled
	// message's arguments were consumed or dropped slot by slot by the arm that ran it and its reply was
	// answered; an abandoned one still holds every payload and still owes its sender an answer, and
	// `<T>.__abandon` is what does both — `ServiceError.stopped` into the reply slot, then the
	// tag-conditional `__destruct_<T>.request`, which is the only thing that knows what the payloads are.
	// That is the design's own argument for a closed union: *a closure-based mailbox holds erased
	// environments, cannot know what to free, and would fail the leak gate.*
	//
	// ⚠ It goes through the MAILBOX's own pointer rather than naming the symbol — see `MboxAbandonName` for
	// the E3004 that forced it and for why one door is better than two anyway.
	let abandonOne = fn.openBlock(b"abandon")
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(MboxAbandonName, args: twoArgs(ServiceMailboxParam, second: leftover), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(abandonOne, op: MaxonOp.branch(drain.id), range: range)

	let teardown = fn.openBlock(b"finished")
	fn.terminateBlock(drain, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(drained, thenBlockId: teardown.id, elseBlockId: abandonOne.id), range: range)

	// The state was MOVED into this thread at the `spawn` (`Parser.moveIntoGreenThread`), so this frame is
	// its one owner and this is the drop that pairs with that move.
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(managedFieldDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: project.typeNames, fieldType: stateType), args: argsOf(ServiceStateParam), range: range)
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(MboxServiceExitName, args: argsOf(ServiceMailboxParam), range: range)
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: range)
end 'buildServiceDrain'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`<T>.__abandon(box)` — WHAT A REQUEST NOBODY WILL HANDLE GETS (SV2), AND IT IS A LIVENESS
// OBLIGATION BEFORE IT IS A MEMORY ONE.** Two roads reach it, and both are races the design admits: a send
// that finds the mailbox already CLOSED (`MailboxRuntime.buildMboxSend`'s abandon arm, on the SENDER's own
// green thread), and the loop's DRAIN, which empties whatever was queued behind a `__shutdown` pill. In both
// the message dies unprocessed — and its awaiter is parked on a reply that will never come.
//
// ```
// tag = load(box, tagOffset)
// switch tag
//   variant with a reply -> __gt_cell_complete(load(box, replySlot), 0, ServiceError.stopped + bias)
//   variant without      -> (nothing)
// destruct: __destruct_<T>.request(box)
// ```
//
// ⭐ **THE ANSWER COMES FIRST AND THE PAYLOAD DROP SECOND, and the order is not arbitrary.** The cascade
// frees what the box owns; the reply slot is a raw integer the cascade skips, but reading it after the box
// has been released would be a use-after-free the day a request union grows a payload the cascade rewrites.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE ORDER TO `buildServiceArm`'s, WHICH RELEASES BEFORE IT PUBLISHES — SAID HERE
// SO NEITHER GETS "FIXED" INTO THE OTHER (SV2 review).** The rule both obey is *nothing this green thread
// still owns may be touched after the publish*. The arm has to WORK for it: a handler may return a record
// whose managed field it built over a message parameter, so the reply and the request box can name one string
// and the box's release has to come first. **This function cannot reach that shape at all** — the value word
// it writes is a literal `0`, so a message dying here hands its awaiter nothing, and the payloads the cascade
// frees are the box's alone. The rule is satisfied vacuously, which is what lets the slot-before-cascade
// constraint above decide the order instead.
//
// ⭐ **THE ERROR WORD IS ALREADY FUSED.** `ServiceError` is the transport member, pinned at
// `ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, so `stopped`'s fused ordinal IS the ordinary scalar flag — no shift, and the same
// number an awaiter's `match e ... stopped` arm dispatches on whether the message throws or not.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS SYNTHESIZED FOR EVERY SERVICE, INCLUDING ONE WHOSE MESSAGES ALL LACK REPLIES**, where its
// switch has nothing to do and it is the union's cascade wearing a name. That is deliberate: the mailbox
// holds ONE `abandonFn` pointer, and a per-service choice between two symbols would be a second place that
// knows which services have replies.
function buildServiceAbandon(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray)
	var paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	paramTypes.push(MaxonType.integer)
	var fn = SynthesizedFunction.open(module, project: project, name: serviceAbandonSymbol(typeName), returnType: MaxonReturnType.void, paramTypes: paramTypes)

	let range = SourceRange.none()
	let requestName = serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName)
	let request = requireServiceRequestLayout(signatures, typeName: typeName)
	let box = ServiceAbandonBoxParam

	let entry = fn.currentBlock()
	let tag = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(tag, addr: box, offset: UnionTagOffset, valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)

	// Opened BEFORE the arms so every arm has a block id to branch to — `buildServiceLoop`'s arrangement, for
	// its reason.
	let destruct = fn.openBlock(b"abandondrop")
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(requestName), args: argsOf(box), range: range)
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: range)

	var armIds = BlockIdArray.create()
	for caseIndex in 0 upto request.caseCount() 'eachVariant'
		armIds.push(buildServiceAbandonArm(fn, signatures: signatures, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, box: box, destruct: destruct, range: range).id)
	end 'eachVariant'

	// The default is the DROP, which is also every arm's exit: the table covers every tag the union has, so
	// the default is unreachable by construction and answering it with the release is the conservative answer
	// to a request nothing recognises — `buildServiceLoop`'s call, for its reason.
	fn.terminateBlock(entry, op: MaxonOp.switch(tag, denseTargetBlockIds: armIds, defaultBlockId: destruct.id), range: range)
end 'buildServiceAbandon'

// One abandon arm: answer this variant's reply if it has one, then fall through to the shared release.
function buildServiceAbandonArm(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, request EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, box ValueId, destruct IrBlock, range SourceRange) returns IrBlock
	let arm = fn.openBlock(request.caseNameAt(caseIndex))
	let payloads = request.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
	match serviceReplySlotIndex(payloads) 'reply'
		absent then break 'reply'
		at then fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(GtCellCompleteName, args: threeArgs(loadServiceReplyCell(fn, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, payloads: payloads, req: box, range: range), second: fn.emitTypedLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), third: fn.emitTypedLiteral(serviceStoppedErrorFlag(signatures), valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)), range: range)
	end 'reply'

	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.branch(destruct.id), range: range)
	return arm
end 'buildServiceAbandonArm'

// `<T>.__abandon`'s one parameter, by the ValueId the parameter numbering reserves for it —
// `ServiceStateParam`'s shape, and for its reason.
let ServiceAbandonBoxParam = 0 as ValueId

// ⭐ **ONE DISPATCH ARM.** Variant 0 is the compiler's `__shutdown` pill and stops the loop; every other is a
// message, and runs its handler.
function buildServiceArm(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, instance ServiceStateInstance, request EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, req ValueId, receive IrBlock, exit IrBlock, range SourceRange) returns IrBlock
	let arm = fn.openBlock(request.caseNameAt(caseIndex))
	let payloads = request.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
	if request.caseNameAt(caseIndex).equals(ServiceShutdownCaseName) 'thePoisonPill'
		// ⭐⭐ **THE PILL'S REPLY IS HANDED TO THE MAILBOX, NOT COMPLETED HERE (SV2), AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES
		// `await h.shutdown()` A REAL RENDEZVOUS.** Completing it in this arm would resume the awaiter while
		// the service still had a queue to drain and a state to drop; parking the cell on the mailbox instead
		// lets `__mbox_service_exit` — the loop's LAST act, after the drain and after the state's drop —
		// answer it. The alternative was a value threaded through the drain LOOP as a block argument, which
		// is a phi carrying one pointer through three blocks to reach the same instant.
		//
		// ⚠ The pill's only payload IS the reply slot, so there is nothing else on the box: the shell decref
		// below is the whole release. Whatever is queued BEHIND it is the exit path's to abandon.
		fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(ServiceMailboxParam, offset: MboxOffShutdownReply, value: loadServiceReplyCell(fn, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, payloads: payloads, req: req, range: range), valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)
		fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(MmDecrefName, args: argsOf(req), range: range)
		fn.terminate(MaxonOp.branch(exit.id), range: range)
		return arm
	end 'thePoisonPill'

	let methodName = request.caseNameAt(caseIndex)
	let callee = qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: methodName)
	// The declaration this variant was built from. Its `paramTypes` count is how many of the variant's
	// payloads are ARGUMENTS (the rest is the reply slot), which is what the loop below is bounded by; the
	// "it cannot miss" invariant is `ServiceCompanions.serviceFirstDeclOf`'s, stated once for all four
	// places that rest on it.
	let decl = serviceFirstDeclOf(signatures.overloadedDeclsOf(callee), key: callee)

	// ⚠ **ONLY THE DECLARED PARAMETERS ARE LOADED, AND THE `__reply` SLOT IS NOT ONE.** A message with a
	// reply carries a trailing integer slot the request union declares and SV1 never fills (the send stores
	// 0). Loading it would hand the handler an argument its declaration does not have.
	var args = ValueIdArray.create()
	args.push(ServiceStateParam)
	var payloadClasses = PayloadClassArray.create()
	for slot in 0 upto decl.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		let field = try payloads.get(slot) otherwise panic("buildServiceArm: `{String.from(request.name)}`'s variant `{String.from(methodName)}` declares {payloads.count()} payload(s) against `{String.from(callee)}`'s {decl.paramTypes.count()} parameter(s) — `ServiceCompanions.serviceRequestPayloads` builds one payload per parameter, plus a trailing reply slot")
		let declared = signatures.payloadTypeOf(field, layout: request, into: signatures.indexTypeNames())
		let class = classifyUnionPayload(signatures, typeNames: signatures.indexTypeNames(), fieldType: declared, slotScopeFilePath: request.declFilePath)
		payloadClasses.push(class)
		let loaded = fn.mintOfType(payloadValueTypeOf(class, declared: declared))
		fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(loaded, addr: req, offset: request.payloadSlotOffset(slot), valueType: payloadStorageOf(class)), range: range)
		args.push(loaded)
	end 'eachParam'

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLER'S RETURN TYPE IS DERIVED ONCE AND THREADED.** The call and the completion must agree
	// about a type this instantiation SUBSTITUTED, and two derivations of one substitution are two chances
	// to disagree about which register the reply's value word came back in.
	let returned = serviceHandlerReturnType(signatures, callee: callee, instance: instance, typeNames: signatures.indexTypeNames())
	let reply = serviceArmReply(fn, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, payloads: payloads, req: req, range: range)
	let outcome = emitServiceHandlerCall(fn, callee: callee, returned: returned, signatures: signatures, args: args, range: range)

	// ⛔⛔⛔ **EVERY RELEASE THIS GREEN THREAD OWES HAPPENS BEFORE THE PUBLISH, AND THAT ORDER IS THE WHOLE
	// REASON THE CALL AND THE COMPLETION ARE TWO FUNCTIONS (SV2 review).** `__gt_cell_complete` stores
	// `completed`, which is the instant the AWAITER may resume — on another M. Anything this loop touches
	// after that line it touches while a second green thread is running, and a reference count in shv2 is a
	// plain load/add/store (`MmRuntime.emitAdjustRefcount`, EC10) precisely because the language guarantees
	// ONE green thread per box.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND THE TWO REALLY CAN NAME ONE BOX.** A message may return a record with a managed FIELD, and a
	// handler may build that record over a message PARAMETER — `Wrapper.of(s)` co-owns `s`, so the string is
	// named by the reply the awaiter now holds AND by this request box, whose reference
	// `dropUnconsumedPayloads` is about to give back. Published first, those two decrements are two green
	// threads stepping one count with no atomic between them. **NOT MEASURED as a failure** — the window is
	// three instructions wide and 6,000 awaited replies at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` 1/2/4/8/12 stayed green — but it
	// is a race by construction, and the ordering that removes it costs nothing. It is the same rule
	// `GtRuntime.emitGtTeardownArrive` states for the teardown word: **every read or release of shared state
	// precedes the arrival that hands it over.**
	dropUnconsumedPayloads(fn, signatures: signatures, callee: callee, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, classes: payloadClasses, args: args, range: range)

	// ⭐ **THE SHELL, AND ONLY THE SHELL.** The payloads were consumed by the handler or dropped by the loop
	// one line up, so the box's own record is all that is left to release — and the box was allocated with
	// the trivial destructor (`Parser.emitBoxAlloc`), which is what makes the plain decref the right entry
	// rather than the cascade the abandon path takes.
	//
	// ⚠ It is safe here rather than after the completion for the reason above, and safe this early because
	// nothing below reads `req`: the cell pointer was loaded into `reply` before the call, and the payloads
	// the drops release are the values `args` already holds.
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(MmDecrefName, args: argsOf(req), range: range)

	emitServiceReplyCompletion(fn, signatures: signatures, callee: callee, returned: returned, outcome: outcome, reply: reply, range: range)
	// ⛔⛔ **THE CURRENT BLOCK, NOT `arm` — AND THE TWO ARE NOT THE SAME BLOCK FOR EVERY MESSAGE.** A THROWING
	// handler is a `tryCall` whose error edge is a diamond (`emitServiceReplyCompletion`), so by this line the
	// ops above have landed in that diamond's MERGE and `arm` was terminated by its `condBranch` several lines
	// ago. Terminating `arm` a second time OVERWROTE that branch: the handler still ran (its op precedes the
	// terminator) and control went straight back to `receive`, skipping the payload drops and the shell decref
	// this line and the two above it are.
	//
	// **MEASURED as exit 101 on a two-message program whose second message throws** — correct answers printed,
	// every request box and every moved-in payload of a throwing message stranded. `arm` is still what this
	// function RETURNS, because that is the block the switch jumps to; what it TERMINATES is wherever the ops
	// ended up. `fn.terminate` is that distinction, and it exists on the carrier for exactly this reason.
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.branch(receive.id), range: range)
	return arm
end 'buildServiceArm'

// ⭐ **WHERE THIS ARM'S REPLY GOES, OR THAT IT HAS NONE (SV2).** `serviceReplySlotIndex` is the one
// authority on which variants carry a cell; this loads it where there is one.
function serviceArmReply(fn SynthesizedFunction, request EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, payloads PayloadFieldArray, req ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ServiceReplyTarget
	match serviceReplySlotIndex(payloads) 'reply'
		absent then return ServiceReplyTarget.fireAndForget
		at then return ServiceReplyTarget.cell(loadServiceReplyCell(fn, request: request, caseIndex: caseIndex, payloads: payloads, req: req, range: range))
	end 'reply'
end 'serviceArmReply'

// The cell pointer out of this variant's `__reply` payload slot. Asked only where the slot exists, so the
// panic names a caller that skipped its own gate.
function loadServiceReplyCell(fn SynthesizedFunction, request EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, payloads PayloadFieldArray, req ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let slot = match serviceReplySlotIndex(payloads) 'reply'
		absent panic("loadServiceReplyCell: variant `{String.from(request.caseNameAt(caseIndex))}` of `{String.from(request.name)}` carries no `__reply` payload — every caller gates on `serviceReplySlotIndex`")
		at(index) gives index
	end 'reply'

	let cell = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(cell, addr: req, offset: request.payloadSlotOffset(slot), valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)
	return cell
end 'loadServiceReplyCell'

// ⭐ **WHAT AN ARM OWES ITS SENDER.** A union rather than a `ValueId` plus a flag, for `ServiceReplyValue`'s
// reason one tier up: a fire-and-forget message has no cell at all, and `v0` is a real SSA value.
union ServiceReplyTarget
	fireAndForget
	cell(value ValueId)
end 'ServiceReplyTarget'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THE HANDLER PRODUCED, BEFORE ANY OF IT IS PUBLISHED (SV2 review).** The two shapes a message
// can have, carried from the call to the completion across the releases that must sit between them — see
// `buildServiceArm` for why that gap exists and what closing it would cost.
union ServiceHandlerOutcome
	// A non-throwing handler: one result register, which is `void` for a message that returns nothing.
	answered(result ValueId)
	// A throwing handler: the result register and the RAW error flag its `tryCall` set — raw, because the
	// shift onto the reply's fused line belongs with the completion that writes the word.
	routed(result ValueId, errorFlag ValueId)
end 'ServiceHandlerOutcome'

// ⭐⭐ **THE HANDLER CALL, AND WHAT BECOMES OF ITS TWO OUTCOMES (SV2).** A message with a reply hands both
// to the CELL and drops neither: the awaiter takes the value's reference (`__gt_try_await` releases the
// arguments with the result CONSUMED) and reads the error word. A message WITHOUT a reply returns nothing
// and throws nothing by construction — that is exactly what `serviceMessageHasAReply` answers `false` for —
// so its call has no outcome to place at all.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THAT IS WHY THREE DROP HELPERS LEFT THIS FILE.** SV1 dropped a fire-and-forget message's managed
// RESULT, its thrown ERROR BOX, and the result again on the non-throwing edge — all three reachable only
// because a value-returning or throwing message had nowhere to send its answer. Every such message now has
// a cell, so all three became unreachable, and an unreachable drop in a leak-gated compiler is a claim
// nothing can check. (The one that would still be reachable — a thrown BOX — cannot arrive either: a
// message throwing a payload-carrying union is refused at the `spawn`, E3140, precisely because a reply's
// one error word holds a fused ordinal and not a pointer.)
//
// ⛔ **A `tryCall` OPENS AN ERROR-EDGE DIAMOND, so the ops after this call land in that diamond's MERGE
// rather than in the arm's own block** — which `buildServiceArm` did not account for at SV1 wave 3 and paid
// exit 101 for. See the terminator at the end of `buildServiceArm` for what that cost and why it is
// `fn.terminate`.
//
// A plain `call` to a throwing callee is **E3057** at `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall` (which walks this
// synthesized body like any other), so the `tryCall` is FORCED.
function emitServiceHandlerCall(fn SynthesizedFunction, callee ByteArray, returned MaxonReturnType, signatures ProgramSignatures, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange) returns ServiceHandlerOutcome
	let resultTag = match returned 'r'
		void gives ValueTypeTag.void
		value(inner) gives maxonTypeTag(inner)
	end 'r'
	let resultNameId = match returned 'n'
		void gives UnnamedTypeId
		value(inner) gives typeNameIdOf(inner)
	end 'n'

	if not throwsClauseIsThrowing(signatures.throwsOf(callee)) 'nonThrowing'
		return ServiceHandlerOutcome.answered(fn.emitPositionalCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: resultNameId, range: range))
	end 'nonThrowing'

	let result = fn.mint(resultTag, nameId: resultNameId)
	let errorFlag = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.tryCall(result, errorFlag: errorFlag, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: emptyArgLabels(args), argRanges: uniformArgRanges(args, range: range), argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, argDefaultSlots: NoDefaultArgSlots, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted), range: range)
	return ServiceHandlerOutcome.routed(result, errorFlag: errorFlag)
end 'emitServiceHandlerCall'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE ANSWER — THE LAST THING THIS ARM DOES TO A STRUCTURE ANYBODY ELSE CAN SEE (SV2
// review).** `__gt_cell_complete` publishes `completed`, which is the instant the awaiter may resume on
// another M; `buildServiceArm` states what that costs when a release comes after it, and why every one of
// them is emitted above this call.
function emitServiceReplyCompletion(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, callee ByteArray, returned MaxonReturnType, outcome ServiceHandlerOutcome, reply ServiceReplyTarget, range SourceRange)
	match outcome 'o'
		answered(result) then completeServiceReply(fn, reply: reply, result: serviceReplyResultWord(fn, returned: returned, result: result, range: range), threw: fn.emitTypedLiteral(NoErrorFlag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), range: range)
		routed(result, errorFlag) then emitRoutedReplyCompletion(fn, signatures: signatures, callee: callee, returned: returned, result: result, errorFlag: errorFlag, reply: reply, range: range)
	end 'o'
end 'emitServiceReplyCompletion'

// The THROWING handler's two edges. Split from the arm above because a `match` arm is one statement and this
// is a diamond.
function emitRoutedReplyCompletion(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, callee ByteArray, returned MaxonReturnType, result ValueId, errorFlag ValueId, reply ServiceReplyTarget, range SourceRange)
	let threw = fn.emitCompare(errorFlag, rhs: fn.emitTypedLiteral(NoErrorFlag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), op: MaxonCmpOp.ne, range: range)
	let caught = fn.currentBlock()

	// ⭐⭐ **THE THROWN EDGE WRITES THE FUSED ORDINAL, NOT THE RAW FLAG.** A reply's one error word carries a
	// `(member, case)` pair on a two-member dispatch line, and the handler's own flag is the SECOND member's
	// half of it — so the shift `ServiceCompanions.serviceReplyHandlerFusedShift` computes is added here, and
	// `Parser.serviceReplyErrorMembers` builds the awaiter's table from the very same offset.
	let thrown = fn.openBlock(b"replythrew")
	completeServiceReply(fn, reply: reply, result: fn.emitTypedLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), threw: emitFusedHandlerFlag(fn, signatures: signatures, callee: callee, errorFlag: errorFlag, range: range), range: range)

	// ⚠ On the THROWN path the success register holds whatever the exit left there — the undefined slot
	// `AsyncReleaser`'s header calls a wild free — so the VALUE word is written only on the edge that really
	// produced one, and the thrown edge writes a 0 the releaser's null guard reads as "nothing was put here".
	let answered = fn.openBlock(b"replyok")
	completeServiceReply(fn, reply: reply, result: serviceReplyResultWord(fn, returned: returned, result: result, range: range), threw: fn.emitTypedLiteral(NoErrorFlag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), range: range)

	let handled = fn.openBlock(b"handled")
	fn.terminateBlock(caught, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(threw, thenBlockId: thrown.id, elseBlockId: answered.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(thrown, op: MaxonOp.branch(handled.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(answered, op: MaxonOp.branch(handled.id), range: range)
	fn.resumeBlock(handled)
end 'emitRoutedReplyCompletion'

// One reply answered, or nothing at all for a fire-and-forget message. Written once because both edges of a
// throwing call and the whole of a non-throwing one reach it, and the ORDER of the three words
// `__gt_cell_complete` writes is the runtime's to state, not this file's.
function completeServiceReply(fn SynthesizedFunction, reply ServiceReplyTarget, result ValueId, threw ValueId, range SourceRange)
	match reply 'r'
		fireAndForget then return
		cell(value) then fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(GtCellCompleteName, args: threeArgs(value, second: result, third: threw), range: range)
	end 'r'
end 'completeServiceReply'

// The VALUE word a reply carries: the handler's result, or a 0 for a message that returns nothing. A void
// call names no value at all, so it cannot be passed as an argument — the literal is what says "there is
// nothing here" in a word that must always be written.
function serviceReplyResultWord(fn SynthesizedFunction, returned MaxonReturnType, result ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match returned 'r'
		void gives fn.emitTypedLiteral(0, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		value gives result
	end 'r'
end 'serviceReplyResultWord'

// The handler's raw error flag, shifted onto the reply's fused dispatch line. A shift of 0 emits nothing,
// which is what a first-member placement would give — but the handler is always the SECOND member here, so
// the zero arm is the structural case rather than the common one.
function emitFusedHandlerFlag(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, callee ByteArray, errorFlag ValueId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let errorName = try throwsClauseName(signatures.throwsOf(callee)) otherwise panic("emitFusedHandlerFlag: `{String.from(callee)}` reached the THROWN edge of its own dispatch arm with no `throws` clause — the caller emits that edge only for a callee `throwsClauseIsThrowing` accepted")
	let shift = serviceReplyHandlerFusedShift(signatures, errorName: errorName.toByteArray())
	if shift == 0 'alreadyOnTheLine'
		return errorFlag
	end 'alreadyOnTheLine'

	let fused = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.binOp(fused, lhs: errorFlag, rhs: fn.emitTypedLiteral(shift, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), op: MaxonBinOp.add), range: range)
	return fused
end 'emitFusedHandlerFlag'

// ⭐⭐ **DROP EVERY MANAGED PAYLOAD THE HANDLER DID NOT TAKE OWNERSHIP OF.** The request box holds exactly
// one reference to each of its managed payloads — the one `Parser.moveIntoGreenThread` moved in at the send
// — and the handler either consumed it or borrowed it. A borrowed one is still this loop's to release, and
// releasing it is what balances the move.
//
// ⚠ **`paramIsConsumed` ANSWERS `false` FOR EVERY MESSAGE, AND THAT IS A CHECKED ANSWER, NOT A SKIP.**
// The sweep reads every declaration's parameters, an instance method's included, but only three doors can
// mark one consumed and a message handler can hold none of them: a direct `Self{f: p}` store in an instance
// method is co-ownership rather than a move (`Parser.scanBodyFacts`, `detectFieldStores: false`); a
// type-parameter feed needs a payload typed at one, and a promise reclaim needs a `Promise` payload — and
// `untransferableParameterNoun` refuses BOTH at the `spawn` (E3135), reading the handler's declared slot
// type: a send cannot know an opaque `T`'s layout, and a promise owns a green thread its awaiter holds.
// Asking the ONE door rather than hard-coding that costs a map lookup per payload at compile time and is
// what keeps this correct the day a fourth door opens; hard-coding it would be a double free that day.
//
// ⚠ The index is CALL-ALIGNED — the receiver is position 0 — which is the space `ParamConsumeInfo`'s columns
// are recorded in (`Parser.recordScannedSignature` states it), and `args` is built in that same order.
function dropUnconsumedPayloads(fn SynthesizedFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, callee ByteArray, request EnumLayout, caseIndex CaseIndex, classes PayloadClassArray, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
	let payloads = request.payloadsAt(caseIndex)
	for slot in 0 upto classes.count() 'eachPayload'
		let class = try classes.get(slot) otherwise panic("dropUnconsumedPayloads: classes.get OOB at {slot} — one class is pushed per loaded payload")
		if not payloadClassIsManaged(class) 'ownsNoHeap'
			continue
		end 'ownsNoHeap'
		if signatures.paramIsConsumed(callee, index: (slot + ServiceReceiverArgCount) as ParamCount) 'theHandlerTookIt'
			continue
		end 'theHandlerTookIt'

		let field = try payloads.get(slot) otherwise panic("dropUnconsumedPayloads: payloads.get OOB at {slot} — one class was pushed per payload read out of this same list")
		let loaded = try args.get(slot + ServiceReceiverArgCount) otherwise panic("dropUnconsumedPayloads: args.get OOB at {slot + ServiceReceiverArgCount} — `args` holds the receiver followed by one value per payload")
		fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(managedFieldDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: signatures.indexTypeNames(), fieldType: signatures.payloadTypeOf(field, layout: request, into: signatures.indexTypeNames())), args: argsOf(loaded), range: range)
	end 'eachPayload'
end 'dropUnconsumedPayloads'

// `args[0]` of a message dispatch is the service's own state — the receiver, which fills no payload slot. So
// payload `j` is `args[j + ServiceReceiverArgCount]`, exactly as `MaxonDialect.WitnessReceiverArgCount`
// states the same kind of fact for a witness dispatch.
let ServiceReceiverArgCount = 1 as ParamCount

// The request union's layout, or a panic: a service's companions were minted before the parse
// (`ServiceCompanions.synthesizeServiceCompanions`), so a service whose union is missing here is a compiler
// bug rather than a program's. The LOOKUP is `ServiceCompanions.serviceRequestLayoutOf`'s — see there for
// why it throws and its knowing callers panic in their own words.
function requireServiceRequestLayout(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray) returns EnumLayout
	return try serviceRequestLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName) otherwise panic("requireServiceRequestLayout: nothing declares `union {String.from(serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName))}` — the companions are minted for every accepted spawn target inside `queryProgramSignatures`, before any body is parsed")
end 'requireServiceRequestLayout'

// A handler's return type AS THIS LOOP'S INSTANTIATION FIXES IT, `void` for an undeclared callee — which is
// a program SemanticCheck rejects with E3004, reached here only because this synthesis runs over a module
// that may still be doomed. The substitution is what gives a `returns T` handler's result the same concrete
// tag the awaiter reads it back at; the reply cell's own type was substituted through the same instance at
// the send (`SignatureIndex.internPromiseInstanceForReply`).
function serviceHandlerReturnType(signatures ProgramSignatures, callee ByteArray, instance ServiceStateInstance, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns MaxonReturnType
	return match signatures.returnTypeOf(callee, into: typeNames) 'r'
		declared(declaredReturn) gives serviceInstanceResolvedReturn(signatures, returnType: declaredReturn, declFilePath: signatures.recordedReturnDeclFileOf(callee), instance: instance)
		undeclared gives MaxonReturnType.void
	end 'r'
end 'serviceHandlerReturnType'
